                <pb facs="0601" n="499" sameAs="frus:frus1919v01/pg499"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/2524</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of the Third Assistant Secretary of
                        State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Long</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-10-30"><hi rendition="#italic">October 30,
                        1919</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Debuchi asked me this morning if he understood correctly the question of the
                    two railroads mentioned in our recent note on the subject of Japanese
                    reservations to the Consortium. He asked me whether they were considered as
                    parts on which substantial progress had been made. I told him that railroads
                    which were built were not in the Consortium; that extensions to those railroads
                    on which substantial progress had been made were, under the terms of the
                    Consortium, not included; and that railroads in continuation of those built, or
                    upon which substantial progress had been made and which had been already
                    contracted for and projected were to be considered as without the scope of the
                    Consortium under the theory that they were parts of a whole, and that
                    substantial progress had been made upon the whole.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Breckinridge Long</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
